The preparation of MOFs that are redox active and/or based upon metals in nontraditional or difficult to access oxidation states generally relies on the use of air-sensitive starting materials, necessitating the use of rigorously air-free reaction conditions and limiting materials discovery throughput. Moreover, for many metal ions in reduced oxidation states, the number of available starting materials is limited, as the synthesis of a metal−organic framework often relies on screening a number of parameters, including counteranion identity, placing additional bottlenecks on discovery of new structures.23 In addressing these limitations, electrochemical methods present an intriguing alternative strategy for synthesis of reduced MOF materials.
Redox reactivity has been coupled to synthesis of MOFs. For example, redox chemistry has been used to drive ligand deprotonation reactions coupled to MOF syntheses.24 Additionally, electrochemical dissolution of metallic anodes to generate oxidized metal ions (i.e., $\mathbf{M}^{0}\to\mathbf{M}^{n+})$ , has been utilized to synthesize Cu2+ and Zn2+ based MOFs.25−28 Despite these examples, we are aware of no published examples in which electrochemistry has been leveraged to alter the oxidization state of a dissolved metal ion in solution prior to MOF synthesis. Such a strategy is attractive as it can allow for the oxidation state of dissolved ions to be rapidly tuned via controlled potential electrolysis (CPE) for the targeted synthesis of MOFs containing metal centers in nontraditional, reduced oxidation states. Additionally, this strategy permits the large number of high oxidation state metal starting materials that are inexpensive and readily accessible to be converted to higher value reduced forms and facilitate the discovery of new materials containing metal ions in reactive oxidation states. This method also has the advantage that a larger number of reaction conditions can be screened, as they can be set up in the presence of air and then deoxygenated prior to electrochemical reduction. To this end, we targeted the synthesis of novel $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ frameworks given their scarcity and potential for small molecule storage and activation.
The $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ based MOF $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-101 can be prepared by reaction of exogenous ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{3}$ with terephthalic acid $\left(\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\right)$ at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in 10:1 $N,N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF):ethanol for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ under rigorously air-free conditions. In order to prepare this material from $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ generated electrochemically from $\bar{\mathrm{Ti}}^{4+}$ sources, we utilized cyclic voltammetry (CV) to determine potentials (Figures S1−S3) at which $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ can be generated from a series of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ based sources (e.g., $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4},$ ${\bar{\mathrm{Ti}}}(\mathrm{O}^{i}\mathrm{Pr})_{4},$ $\mathrm{Cp}_{2}\mathrm{TiCl}_{2},$ ). In particular, CVs recorded for solutions of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ (Figure 2a) showed a cathodic wave corresponding to $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ reduction at $E_{\mathrm{cat}}=-1.20\:\mathrm{V}$ (all potentials reported versus $\mathrm{Ag/}$ $\mathrm{\sfAgNO}_{3},$ . Preparative scale reduction of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ by CPE at $E_{\mathrm{applied}}$ $=-1.20\mathrm{~V~}$ using a nickel electrode could be monitored by UV−vis spectroscopy (Figure $\mathsf{2b},\mathsf{c}$ ). The complete reduction of starting $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ to $\bar{\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ was accomplished in as little as $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ . $\mathtt{A4h}$ CPE $\mathrm{'}E_{\mathrm{applied}}=-1.20\mathrm{V})$ of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ in the presence $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}$ and $0.1\mathrm{~M~TBAPF}_{6}$ as supporting electrolyte in a 10:1 DMF/ EtOH mixture using a nickel foam working electrode, followed by heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ afforded a crystalline dark purple powder in high yield.
Powder X-ray diffraction experiments indicated the resulting material is isostructural to the previously reported $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL101 phase (Figure S6). The structure is comprised of trimeric $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ clusters connected to form tetrahedral building units with $\operatorname{bdc}^{2-}$ anions on their vertices. The tetrahedral units are edge linked to afford a structure containing two large mesoporous cages, one with both pentagonal and hexagonal windows and the other comprised solely of pentagonal windows. $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL101 obtained via the previously reported solvothermal method from starting $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ displays a BET (Langmuir) surface area of 2970 (4440) $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g}$ .22 The corresponding material synthesized via electrochemical generation of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ from $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ has a BET (Langmuir) surface area of 3285 (4360) $\mathbf{m}^{2}/\mathbf{g}$ . Although PXRD, SEM, XPS, and TGA analyses indicate the solvothermally and electrochemically generated materials are virtually indistinguishable, synthesis of the latter is accomplished using inexpensive $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4},$ as opposed to relying on exogenously prepared and highly air-sensitive ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{3}$ . Of further note is the fact that commercial $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ is routinely available in $99.9\%$ (and higher) purity, while $\mathrm{TiCl}_{3}$ is typically prepared via aluminum reduction that result in $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ starting materials containing appreciable levels of $\mathbb{A}^{3+}$ .
A particular advantage of electrochemically synthesized reduced metal frameworks is the management of proton balance throughout the reaction. Significant concentrations of $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ are generated during the course of solvothermal MOF syntheses as ligands are deprotonated to form $\scriptstyle\mathbf{M}-\mathbf{L}$ bonds. As many MOF syntheses are dependent on $\mathrm{\pH}_{\mathrm{\ell}}$ , the continually increasing proton concentration can complicate synthesis of phase-pure materials. The electrosynthetic strategy described above provides a convenient means to manage $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ build up associated with formation of the bdc2− anions needed for TiIIIMIL-101 synthesis. The nickel foam working electrodes used to generate the $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ from $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ show excellent HER activity at significantly lower potentials than those used for the reduction of $\mathrm{\dot{T}i^{4+}}$ .29 Accordingly, CPE of the $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}/\mathrm{H}_{2}^{\cdot\cdot}$ bdc solutions with the nickel cathode not only generates $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ , but also reduces the carboxylic acid protons to generate the $\operatorname{bdc}^{2-}$ ligands and $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ which is evolved from the cathode compartment.
To follow the production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ over the course of the CPE, we monitored the reaction headspace during $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-101 electrosynthesis via gas chromatography.30,31 Initial formation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was detected within as little as $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{min}}$ and plateaued after ${\sim}3{\mathrm{-}}5\mathrm{~h}$ . Initial Faradaic efficiencies for HER were $<20\%$ and slowly increased to $\sim70\%$ as concentrations of $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ were depleted to generate $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ . In accounting for both the reduction of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ to $\mathbf{\bar{T}i}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{b}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}$ (or $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc},$ vide infra) protons to $\operatorname{H}_{2},$ we consistently observed net current efficiencies in excess of $75\%$ over the course of the entire CPE.
Given the potentially broad applicability of MOF electrosynthesis from soluble metal precursors, we sought to employ this method to expand the library of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ MOFs by targeting other multitopic carboxylate-based ligands. Since the $\mu_{3}–\mathrm{O}$ centered trimeric cluster is ubiquitous to a large number of MIL-type MOFs, we targeted $\mathrm{H}_{3}$ btc-based MIL-100. This structure has previously been reported for trivalent ${\mathrm{Al}},{}^{32}{\mathrm{Sc}},{}^{33}$ ${\mathrm{V}},^{34}{\mathrm{Cr}},^{35}{\mathrm{~\Delta~}}\dot{\mathrm{Fe}},^{36}$ and mixed-metal systems and features connectivity that is analogous to the MIL-101 structure type, but where ligands occupy sites on the faces rather than vertices of the tetrahedral building units. The electrochemical reduction of a $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}/\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc}$ DMF:EtOH (10:1) solution containing $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ $\mathrm{TBAPF}_{6}$ followed by reaction at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ afforded the target material, as confirmed by powder X-ray diffraction (Figure 3). Interestingly, identical solvothermal reaction conditions utilizing commercial $\mathrm{TiCl}_{3}$ rather than electrochemically reduced $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ afforded MOF powders with significantly lower uniformity (Figure 3). After thorough solvent exchanges and activation at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the solvothermally and electrochemically prepared materials showed BET (Langmuir) surface areas of 1304 (1568) and 1736 (2708) $\mathrm{in}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ respectively (Figure 3a). The latter value is in good agreement with those reported for analogous MIL-100 materials.33,35
Despite several attempts, solvothermal syntheses utilizing exogenous ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{3}$ as starting material consistently afforded frameworks with lower surface areas compared to those obtained using $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ that had been electrochemically generated from $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ . The lower surface areas obtained for the solvothermal materials may coincide with a $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ -based framework with MIL-100 topology exhibiting a BET surface area of $1321~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ that was recently reported.37 We note that XPS analysis of solvothermally prepared samples of both $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}.$ - MIL-101 and $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100 show that each retain chloride as a charge-balancing anion in the frameworks. Electrochemically prepared samples of these two $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ -based frameworks both contain fluoride anions (in place of chloride), which is presumed to originate from the $\mathrm{TBAPF}_{6}$ supporting electrolyte.
To determine whether the presence of supporting electrolyte improved the surface area of the electrochemically prepared $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{\hat{III}}}$ -MIL-100, $\mathrm{TBAPF}_{6}$ was added to the solvothermal reaction mixture (exogenous $\mathrm{TiCl}_{3}/\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btc})$ . The thusly obtained dark brown product was both amorphous and nonporous. Further, solvothermal syntheses with exogenous $\mathrm{TiCl}_{3}/\mathrm{H}_{3}|$ btc consistently afforded materials of smaller particle size (Figure 3d) as compared to syntheses utilizing electrochemically reduced $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}^{-}$ (Figure 3c).
Given the near complete dearth of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ -based metal−organic frameworks, we sought to further expand the scope of this method to extended pore structures. Although materials of this type based on the $\mathbf{M}_{3}\mathbf{O}$ building block have been reported, they typically feature surface areas that are significantly lower than values that are calculated for pristine structures. MIL101(Fe)_BPDC, which is a biphenyldicarboxylate-based material that adopts the MIL-101 structure type, displays a BET surface area of just $210\mathrm{~\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ as compared to the theoretical value of $4500~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.\mathrm{\Omega}^{38}$ A MIL-100 structure based on an expanded tritopic $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{btb}\right)$ linker displays a BET surface area of just $26~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ versus the expected value of $3990~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ 38 .
By adopting the straightforward electrosynthetic strategy developed for $\mathrm{\overline{{Ti}}^{I I I}{-}M I L{-}\widetilde{l}O1}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100 (vide supra), we have been able to isolate high-quality extended pore $\mathrm{\bar{\r}i}^{3+}$ based MOFs for the first time. Electrochemical reduction of a $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}$ solution containing either $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bpdc}}\right)$ or $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -[1,3,5]triazine- $^{2,4,6}$ -triyl-tris-benzoic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{tatb}\right)$ followed by heating at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ afforded $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ - MIL-101-bpdc and $\mathrm{\dot{T}i^{I I I}}$ -MIL-100-tatb, respectively. $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL101-bpdc displays a BET surface area of $3\bar{2}63\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\bar{\mathrm{g}}$ and a pore volume of $2.{\overset{-}{2}}0\ {\mathrm{cm}}^{3}/{\mathrm{g}}.$ . As shown in Table S2, these values are ${\sim}50\%$ higher than those realized for samples of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-101- bpdc prepared solvothermally from exogenous $\mathrm{TiCl}_{3}$ and $\bar{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{bpd\bar{c}}$ $(\hat{2}139\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $1.32~\mathrm{{cm}}^{3}/\mathrm{{g}},$ ). Similarly, electrochemically synthesized $\dot{\mathrm{\Ti}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100-tatb displays a BET surface area and pore volume of $5842~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $4.04~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (Figure 4). These values dwarf those realized for analogous solvothermally prepared samples $(740\ \mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.49~\mathrm{\bar{c}m}^{3}/$ g). The pore volume of the electrosynthesized $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100- tatb (note: this structure is also referred to as $\mathrm{PCN}{-}333)^{21}$ is in excellent agreement with the value reported for the analogous Al-based MOF, PCN-333 $\left(3.81~\mathrm{cm}^{3}\right)^{\bullet}\mathrm{g})$ .39 The electrochemically synthesized materials also feature larger and more welldefined particle sizes (Figure 4) and increased crystallinities (Figures S10, S11).
In conclusion, we have leveraged electrochemical methods to synthesize reduced metal−organic frameworks. Electrochemical synthesis of $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-101, $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100, $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL101-bpdc, and $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100-tatb using the inexpensive, accessible, and highly pure $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+}$ -based starting material, $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4},$ generated frameworks with crystallinities and adsorption properties that are as good or superior to those of materials synthesized using exogenous/commercial ${\mathrm{TiCl}}_{3}$ . The electrochemically mediated synthetic strategy we have developed has provided the first reported route to $\dot{\mathrm{Ti}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100 and to highquality samples of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ -based MOFs with extended pore structures and surface areas that approach $6000~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}/\mathrm{{g}}$ .
The electrosynthetic methods disclosed in this report not only generate the high-purity $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ ions from which $\hat{\mathrm{Ti}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL101 and $\mathrm{Ti}^{\mathrm{III}}$ -MIL-100 structure types are built, but also provide a means to manage solution $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{\ell}}}$ , as the $\mathbf{H}^{+}$ ions generated upon formation of the frameworks’ $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{C-}\mathrm{Ar}$ bonds are readily reduced and evolved as $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ gas. Accordingly, the methods outlined herein provide a new strategy for the electrochemically mediated synthesis of new redox-active metal−organic frameworks and for the isolation of reduced MOF materials.
